<main>
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="printhead"><h2>{{title}}</h2><p id="printsetup"></p><p id="printurl">{{printUrl}}</p><p id="printespon">European data: © ESPON · Origin of data: ESPON EGTC. The interpretation of ESPON material does not necessarily reflect the opinion of the ESPON Monitoring Committee. American data: U.S. Census Bureau, American Community Survey 2020–2024, and Freddie Mac.</p></div>
  <p class="crumb"><a href="/research/">← Research</a></p>

  <header class="hero">
    <img class="cover" src="{{up}}../images/research/housing-page.webp"
         alt="{{imageAlt}}" width="1600" height="560" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
    <div class="htop">
      <h1>{{title}}</h1>
      <button type="button" class="sharebtn primary" data-share title="Copy a link that reopens this exact setup">{{svgShare}}Share this setup</button>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">A third of the average income buys fewer than 50 square metres for <b>44 % of Europeans</b>. The same sentence cannot be written about the United States at all: no American public source measures the floor area of housing. So the American half of this page answers in years of income and in rent — and says exactly where the square metre stops, and why.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Europe: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/17445647.2026.2695777" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sielker &amp; Banabak, TU Wien, <i>Journal of Maps</i> 2026</a> · <a href="https://www.espon.eu/projects/access-affordable-and-quality-housing-all-people-house4all" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ESPON HOUSE4ALL</a></span>
      <span>United States: <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Census Bureau, American Community Survey</a> 2020–2024 · <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Freddie Mac PMMS</a></span>
      <span>European listings March 2024 – March 2025, incomes 2024 · American estimates 2020–2024, in 2024 dollars</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="keyrow" aria-label="Key numbers">
    <div class="key"><b>44 %</b><span>of Europeans live where a 30-year mortgage on a third of the average income buys <b>under 50 m²</b></span></div>
    <div class="key"><b>4.1</b><span>years of the median household income is what the median American home costs — and <b>9.7 %</b> of Americans live in a county where it is eight years or more</span></div>
    <div class="key"><b>11,630</b><span>places you can search: <b>8,489</b> European municipalities and <b>3,141</b> US counties, each with its own housing cost, income and mortgage rate</span></div>
    <div class="key"><b>0</b><span>US counties with a published price per square metre: floor area is not collected by any federal survey at that level, so this page leaves it blank rather than guessing</span></div>
  </div>

  <section class="prose intro">
    <p>The question is old and the answer is usually a price index, which tells you nothing you can picture. This page answers in square metres instead: <b>how much space does a third of an income buy here?</b> A third of the money, thirty years of mortgage at the local rate, local prices — and the answer comes out in a unit anyone can stand inside.</p>
    <p>The idea is not mine. In September 2026 Franziska Sielker and Selim Banabak of TU Wien published a pair of maps in the <i>Journal of Maps</i> that did exactly this for every municipality in Europe, from 22 million listings gathered for the ESPON HOUSE4ALL project. Their dataset is the European layer here, reproduced to within a part in a billion. What is new is everything around it: <b>the United States alongside it</b>, built from the Census Bureau's American Community Survey — 3,141 counties, the whole population — and a calculator that answers for <b>your</b> income rather than the local average.</p>
    <p><b>The American half is missing the unit, and that turned out to be the finding.</b> To say how many square metres an income buys you need a price per square metre, and the United States does not publish one. The Census Bureau asks what a home is worth and what the rent is, but never how big it is; the American Housing Survey measures size for about thirty metropolitan areas and no further; new-construction figures stop at four census divisions. County-level floor area exists only inside private listing databases, and none of the companies that hold it grants the right to republish it. So the American map carries the two measures that public data does support — years of income, and rent as a share of income — and the square-metre modes are switched off there rather than filled with a plausible guess.</p>
    <p><b>The two layers are not one ranking, and the page will not pretend otherwise.</b> Europe's income is measured after tax and per adult-equivalent; America's is measured before tax and per household. Europe's prices are what sellers asked for a square metre; America's are what owners think their whole home is worth. Put them in one table by local income and you would be reading the difference between two statistical offices, not between two housing markets. So the map shows one continent at a time and the rankings stay inside it. Type your own income, though, and both sides are answering the identical question — that is the comparison worth having, and it is what the <b>job offer</b> block below is for.</p>
    <p>Everything is computed in your browser from one embedded table: no request leaves the page, and it works from a downloaded file. The <b>Where the numbers come from</b> section says exactly what was taken from where, what was checked, and what could not be made comparable.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── calculator ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="panel" id="p-calc" aria-labelledby="calc-h">
    <div class="phead">
      <div class="pactions"><button type="button" class="ibtn" data-share title="Copy a link to this setup" aria-label="Copy a link to this setup">{{svgShare}}</button></div>
      <h2 id="calc-h">Your numbers</h2>
      <p>Everything here applies to both maps. With no income entered, every place is shown on its own average income — which in Europe reproduces the published maps exactly, and in the United States applies the same terms to the Census Bureau's medians.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="presets" id="presets" role="group" aria-label="Example setups"></div>
    <div class="calc">
      <div class="field f-income">
        <label for="inc">Net income of the household per month</label>
        <div class="inrow">
          <input id="inc" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="50" placeholder="leave empty for the local average">
          <select id="cur" aria-label="Currency"></select>
        </div>
        <p class="fhint" id="inchint">After tax, all earners together. Converted to euro at the ECB reference rate of 11 September 2026.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="share">Share of income for housing <output for="share" id="share-o">a third</output></label>
        <input id="share" type="range" min="20" max="45" step="1" value="33">
        <p class="fhint">The study uses one third. Above 40 % is usually called an overburden.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="term">Mortgage term</label>
        <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Mortgage term in years" id="term">
          <button type="button" data-v="10">10 y</button><button type="button" data-v="15">15 y</button><button type="button" data-v="20">20 y</button><button type="button" data-v="25">25 y</button><button type="button" data-v="30" aria-pressed="true">30 y</button>
        </div>
        <p class="fhint">The study's maps use 30 years; its Annex B repeats them for 20.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="rate">Interest rate, % per year</label>
        <div class="inrow">
          <input id="rate" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="25" step="0.05" placeholder="each place's own national rate">
          <button type="button" class="minibtn" id="rate-x" hidden>national</button>
        </div>
        <p class="fhint" id="ratehint">Empty means each country's own rate: the study's national fixed rates in Europe — 3.01 % in France, 4.00 % in Germany, 8.32 % in Poland — and the Freddie Mac 30-year average for 2024 of 6.72 % in the United States.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="dep">Down payment <output for="dep" id="dep-o">0 %</output></label>
        <input id="dep" type="range" min="0" max="40" step="5" value="0">
        <p class="fhint">Of the price. The study assumes none; the loan then covers the rest. European prices already include transfer tax and notary fees, American ones do not — see the sources below.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label>Household</label>
        <div class="inrow hh">
          <span class="stepper"><button type="button" data-hh="adults" data-d="-1" aria-label="Fewer adults">−</button><b id="adults-o">1</b><button type="button" data-hh="adults" data-d="1" aria-label="More adults">+</button><i>adults</i></span>
          <span class="stepper"><button type="button" data-hh="kids" data-d="-1" aria-label="Fewer children">−</button><b id="kids-o">0</b><button type="button" data-hh="kids" data-d="1" aria-label="More children">+</button><i>children under 14</i></span>
        </div>
        <p class="fhint" id="hhhint">Equivalence factor 1.0 (OECD-modified scale). Used only for the line comparing your income with the local average.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="field f-want">
        <label for="want">I want at least <output for="want" id="want-o">—</output></label>
        <input id="want" type="range" min="0" max="150" step="5" value="0">
        <p class="fhint">Zero switches the filter off. With a value, the map dims every place where that size is out of reach, and the rankings show only the places where it is not.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="setupline" id="setupline" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── map ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="panel" id="p-map" aria-labelledby="map-h">
    <div class="phead">
      <div class="pactions"><button type="button" class="ibtn" data-share title="Copy a link to this setup" aria-label="Copy a link to this setup">{{svgShare}}</button><button type="button" class="ibtn" data-png="map" title="Copy the map as a PNG" aria-label="Copy the map as a PNG">{{svgPng}}</button><button type="button" class="ibtn" data-png-dl="map" title="Download the map as a PNG" aria-label="Download the map as a PNG">{{svgDl}}</button><button type="button" class="ibtn" data-print="map" title="Print the map" aria-label="Print the map">{{svgPrint}}</button></div>
      <h2 id="map-h">The map</h2>
      <p>Two maps, one scale each: <b>Europe</b> by municipality and the <b>United States</b> by county. They are shown one at a time because the incomes underneath them are measured differently — the <a href="#sources">sources</a> section is blunt about it. Countries are filled with their population-weighted average; each dot is a place, coloured on the same scale. Hover or tap either for its numbers, zoom in for more places, and search to jump straight to one — the search covers both maps and switches between them.</p>
      <p>The last two measures answer the questions people actually ask. <b>Years of income</b> is how many years of income the home costs outright, and <b>rent share</b> is what renting it takes out of a month. In Europe both follow the size you set under "I want", because there a price per square metre exists. In the United States they are measured on the county's own median home and median rental instead, because floor area is not published — the legend says which basket it is using, and the three square-metre modes are switched off in that frame rather than drawn as an empty map.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bar">
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Which map" id="frame"></div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="What the map shows" id="mapmode">
        <button type="button" data-v="buy" aria-pressed="true">m² to buy</button><button type="button" data-v="rent" aria-pressed="false">m² to rent</button><button type="button" data-v="diff" aria-pressed="false">Rent − buy</button><button type="button" data-v="years" aria-pressed="false">Years of income</button><button type="button" data-v="share" aria-pressed="false">Rent share</button>
      </div>
      <p class="fhint" id="modehint" hidden>The three square-metre modes are off here: no US source publishes the floor area of housing by county, so years of income and rent share are measured on the county's own median home instead. <a href="#sources">Where the numbers come from</a>.</p>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Whose income" id="basis">
        <button type="button" data-v="local" aria-pressed="true">Local average income</button><button type="button" data-v="mine" aria-pressed="false" disabled title="Enter your income above">My income</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="gsearch">
      <label class="sr-only" for="q">Search a town, region or country</label>
      <input id="q" type="text" placeholder="Town, county, region or country — e.g. Porto, Bavaria, Travis County" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" role="combobox" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qsug" aria-autocomplete="list">
      <button type="button" class="gq-clear" id="qx" aria-label="Clear search" hidden>×</button>
      <ul class="gsug" id="qsug" role="listbox" aria-label="Matching places" hidden></ul>
    </div>
    <div class="mapwrap">
      <div class="mapbox" id="mapbox">
        <svg id="map" viewBox="0 0 {{geoW}} {{geoH}}" role="img" aria-label="Map: countries filled and places marked as dots, coloured by affordable square metres">
          <g id="mapg">
            <g id="regions"></g>
            <g id="marks"></g>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <div class="legend" id="legend" aria-label="Map legend"></div>
        <div class="zoombar">
          <button type="button" class="zbtn" id="zin" aria-label="Zoom in">+</button>
          <button type="button" class="zbtn" id="zout" aria-label="Zoom out">−</button>
          <button type="button" class="zbtn" id="zreset" aria-label="Reset the view" title="Reset the view">⌂</button>
        </div>
        <div class="ghint"><span class="ghint-mouse">drag to pan · ctrl + wheel to zoom</span><span class="ghint-touch">drag sideways · pinch to zoom</span></div>
        <div class="tip" id="maptip" role="status"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cardcol" id="card">
        <p class="plabel">Selected place</p>
        <p class="cempty">Search a town above or click a region on the map. The card shows what your settings mean there, and what it would take.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
